  Quote of the Week:

  German philosopher George Hegel, 1821:
  "In England even the poorest of people believe they have Rights; this is 
very different from what satisfies the poor in other lands."


ACTION REPORT    

Here's what happened to Your Majesty this turn:


  In spring, Bradipo I's attempted cast of the Skeletal Army spell was 
unsuccessful.
  The wet spring was followed by a sultry early summer.  Kiska was hit by a 
plague.  The plague struck the 33rd Legion in Kiska and destroyed them.  The 
plague struck the 265th Axemen in Kiska and destroyed them.  The plague 
struck the 290th Phalanx in Kiska and destroyed them.  The plague struck the 
305th Light Cavalry in Kiska and destroyed them.  Bradipo I announced a plea 
for recruits in Kiska and was able to form the 76th Eagles to fight for 
Bradipo I.
  In late summer, Bradipo I's attempted cast of the Flood spell was 
unsuccessful.
  The sultry days of summer mellowed into the fall.  Bradipo I was 
instructed in White Magic by Marlon.  Bradipo I practiced diligently for 
many weeks and his skill in White Magic increased.
  With the coming of the early winter season, Bradipo I practiced well and 
improved his ability in Druidism.

    Bradipo I was visited by a hero from Valhalla, named Ambrosius!    The  
characteristics for this great King from Game 211 are given in your 
"CHARACTERS" report.
  If Ambrosius searches a feature, any monetary treasure Ambrosius captures 
will be given to you.  If Ambrosius captures a magic treasure, Ambrosius 
will give it to your King when he departs for Valhalla, or you can order 
Ambrosius to Give it to one of your characters.  You can do anything you 
want with the magic item Ambrosius came with, but the item will leave at the 
end of his stay with you.  Except for the rules given in this paragraph, 
Ambrosius can generally be treated just like your other Heroes, and can be 
affected by any spell except Resurrection.  The main limitation in using 
Ambrosius is that Ambrosius has only enough patience to practice with you 
for one of the phase(s) Ambrosius is with you.  Ambrosius will leave at the 
end of the last phase Ambrosius is scheduled to serve you, after all battles 
have been fought.  Ambrosius can lead armies, but when Ambrosius returns to 
Valhalla, the armies will normally be left leaderless in the province they 
are in.  If Ambrosius has the armies in a province you don't own, they will 
be transferred to your lowest-numbered leader in the same province, or at 
last resort, the garrison commander in an adjacent province owned by you.  
If no leader is available, the armies will be disbanded.
  Ambrosius can be given orders only for the first 4 phase(s) of the turn.

  Thus endeth the adventures of Bradipo I for this year.


  Bradipo I treated with his ally Lady of Beer Mbaba (#3).  Bradipo I 
treated with his ally Hokuto (#4).  Bradipo I treated with his ally Prometeo 
(#5).  Bradipo I treated with his ally Kahn (#6).  Bradipo I treated with 
his ally Giovanna d'Arco (#7).  Bradipo I treated with his ally Circe (#8).  
Bradipo I treated with his ally Agesilao (#9).
  Your Majesty, I have news to report of wanderers in our kingdom:  
  Tilden (#47) has just recently wandered into Kiska from Kaluga.
  Brunhilda (#86) arose one day with a desire to see Transylvania, and so 
left Mongu to travel to that province.


  Here is the financial report for Bradipo I's kingdom: this turn, the 
Kingdom collected 41 gold in revenue.  2 gold was expended to finance our 
leaders.  The 76th Eagles trained well and improved in quality.  19 gold was 
expended to maintain our armies.  Bradipo I was left with 71 gold in the 
treasury.


LETTER REPORTS

  O King of Kings, thy heroes have travelled around the globe in
search of adventure and conquest.  I have faithfully kept their
correspondence, and now I present it to thee:


From: Adele
To: Bradipo I

  Honored Sovereign, 

  In spring, Brunhilda turned down the offer of employment, telling of her 
disdain for Bradipo I's alignment and criticizing Bradipo I for "Keeping a 
palace court filled with goons and obsequious weasels!"  After talking to 
Brunhilda, I judged her (a Orc well known for Necromancy) to have a Pagan, 
Cautious character.
  The wet spring was followed by a fragrant early summer.  I cast a Flood 
spell and Mongu was magically flooded.
  I heard a rumor telling of a feature in Abu Matariq (#106) previously 
unknown to us, a Catacombs inhabited by the Renegade Priest.  The Catacombs 
is reputed to contain the Book of Druidic Incantations.
  With the coming of the late summer season I gathered my courage and my 
wits and entered the Cave.  I encountered the Renegade Swordsman.  I thought 
to cast a Hand Of Death spell, but dropped a needed material component 
before I could make my attempt.
  The Renegade Swordsman moved into bow range.  I raised my arms and 
gestured mightily, meaning to cast a Fireball spell, but it went awry and a 
dove tumbled out of my sleeve instead.
  The Renegade Swordsman charged into melee range.  Undaunted, 
the Renegade Swordsman swang at me and scored 2 damage points on me.  I 
thrust at the Renegade Swordsman and walloped the Renegade Swordsman with 1 
damage points.  The Renegade Swordsman swang at me and scored on me, 
inflicting 2 points of damage.  I summoned all my will and desperately swang 
at my nemesis and inflicted 6 damage points.
  The Renegade Swordsman circled right and violently assailed me and damaged 
me, scoring 4 damage points.  Hoping to exploit a weak point in 
the Renegade Swordsman's defenses, I deftly swang at the Renegade Swordsman 
and inflicted 1 damage points on the Renegade Swordsman.  Realizing that 
discretion is sometimes the better part of valor, I fled from the dangerous 
combat.
  I saw nothing special while scouting out the Cave.  During my travels, a 
Renegade Necromancer suddenly came at me like a bolt from the blue.  I 
hailed the Renegade Necromancer with respectful words, and combat was 
avoided.

  With the coming of the fall season I travelled to Orielle.  While 
adventuring, I encountered no monsters.
  During early winter, Robin (#51) rejected Adele's offer of employment, 
ridiculing Bradipo I's alignment and saying "That Bradipo I is the most 
tyrannical ruler in the land!"  After talking to Robin, I judged him (a 
Human well known for Psychic Magic) to have an Evil, Berserk character.

  Thy humble servant,   Adele


From: Indra
To: Bradipo I

  Honored Sovereign, 

  Winter melted into spring, and I prepared to carry out my urgent orders.  
I discovered nothing in my sagely experiments.
  With the advent of the early summer season, I discovered nothing in my 
sagely researches.
  In late summer, I practiced my Sageness and improved substantially.  I 
discovered nothing in my sagely researches.
  The summer mellowed into fall.  I tried to practice my Sageness, but the 
task was beyond my natural aptitude.  In my sagely researches, I discovered 
a fabulous invention called the Greek Fire!  The following benefits are 
conferred by the Greek Fire:
  Greek Fire is a special mixture of flammables which sets the wood of enemy 
ships on fire and is very difficult to put out.  Its effect is to raise the 
Naval ability of all of the characters of the Kingdom by 1 level.

  The fall darkened into a frigid early winter.  I tried to practice my 
Sageness, but the task was beyond my ability.  I invented nothing in my 
sagely researches.

  Thy humble servant,   Indra


